{{/*
The defects that break a claim about STATE the subject entered.

Each plants a subject that is right about the state once and wrong about
it afterwards, which is what separates these from the answers family: a
method that always succeeds breaks a claim about refusal, and these
break a claim about what happens NEXT.
*/}}

{{- /*
The after-close claim's defect: one stamped method keeps working past
Close.

Exactly one, not all of them. A double that ignored Close entirely would
red a law probing any method, and would prove nothing about whether the
probe set is wide enough — which is the claim's real content. Leaving
one method alive is what forces a multi-probe law to stay plural: a law
narrowed to any other method goes green against this.
*/ -}}
{{- define "suite.defect.partial-outlive" -}}
{{ renderExpr (external $.Prove "One") }}({{ printf "%q" .DefectName }},
	func(tb {{ renderExpr (external "testing" "TB") }}) {{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Subject) }} {
		return {{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Ctor) }}(tb, {{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Option) }}(
			func{{ renderParams .AnonParams }} {{ renderReturns .NamedReturns }} {
				// Answers whatever it is asked, whenever it is asked. The
				// close that should have poisoned it is somewhere else in
				// the double, doing its job on every other method.
				return
			}))
	}){{ template "suite.frag.defect-reason" . }}
{{- end -}}

{{- /*
The poison claim's defect: the sentinel arrives once and then heals.

The un-sticky poison the law forbids, and the only shape that tests what
the law is for. A subject that never reported the sentinel breaks a
different claim — that the state is REACHED — and one that reports it
forever is correct.
*/ -}}
{{- define "suite.defect.sentinel-once" -}}
{{ renderExpr (external $.Prove "One") }}({{ printf "%q" .DefectName }},
	func(tb {{ renderExpr (external "testing" "TB") }}) {{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Subject) }} {
		reported := false
		return {{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Ctor) }}(tb, {{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Option) }}(
			func{{ renderParams .AnonParams }} {{ renderReturns .NamedReturns }} {
				if !reported {
					reported = true
					{{ .ErrSlot }} = {{ renderExpr .Sentinel }}
					return
				}
				// Healed. The state the sentinel described is still the
				// state it is in, and it has stopped saying so.
				return
			}))
	}){{ template "suite.frag.defect-reason" . }}
{{- end -}}

{{- /*
The monotonic claim's defect: the answered position stops moving.

Frozen rather than decreasing, because frozen is the weaker violation
and therefore the stronger proof: a law that catches a position going
backwards may still miss one that repeats, and a repeat is what a real
appender produces when it forgets to advance.
*/ -}}
{{- define "suite.defect.freeze-return" -}}
{{ renderExpr (external $.Prove "One") }}({{ printf "%q" .DefectName }},
	func(tb {{ renderExpr (external "testing" "TB") }}) {{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Subject) }} {
		return {{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Ctor) }}(tb, {{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Option) }}(
			func{{ renderParams .AnonParams }} {{ renderReturns .NamedReturns }} {
				// Every append lands and every one answers the same
				// position. The writes are real; the accounting is not.
				return
			}))
	}){{ template "suite.frag.defect-reason" . }}
{{- end -}}
